<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">A Vindication of the Rights of Woman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1792">1792</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Vindication of the Rights of Woman</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A Vindication of the Rights of Woman is a 1792 essay by the British philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-wollstonecraft">Mary Wollstonecraft</a>, considered a pioneering work of feminist philosophy. Prompted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-maurice-de-talleyrand">Talleyrand</a>'s 1791 report to the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a>, which proposed only a domestic education for women, it argues that women deserve the same fundamental rights as men and a rational education. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Well</a> received at first, it acquired an aura of ill repute after William Godwin's memoir of 1798 revealed its author's private life. It influenced 19th-century campaigners for women's rights, notably at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-falls-convention">Seneca Falls Convention</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/a-vindication-of-the-rights-of-woman.jpg" alt="A Vindication of the Rights of Woman" width="320" height="521" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mary Wollstonecraft · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vindication1b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
